archmage84 wrote:
>
> Of course, all this is taking common sense into account. You wouldn'd
> advance skills you're not going to use; likewise, Wizards don't need
> Expert in divination. Or many other schools, short of escape and
> attack.

When first playing a class it might not be obvious what schools you're
going to use.  Knowing things like the value of missile skills and the
fact that the major spell change (if any) is going to skilled not to
expert is very good for a first cut.  Comparing weapon skills against
the artifacts you expect to get says something about the DevTeam's
intent.

> That's good to hear though. I've got a good (if not a little basic)
> idea of what I should be doing with skills.

Once I start playing a specific class a lot I write up a table and from
that I get a general plan of what's I'll want to enhance.  Sometimes
game survival dicates otherwise (I've needed to enhance a wizard's
quarterstaff but I never desire to do so) but having a plan leads to
better game play.


